1. Overview
The Contract Intelligence Platform is being developed to transform complex provider contracts into structured, searchable, and accessible business knowledge.

Provider contract information is often distributed across thousands of PDF documents, amendments, exhibits, rate schedules, and manually maintained references. Answering a contract-related question may require users to locate the correct document, review multiple amendments, interpret complex contractual language, and reconcile information from several sources.

The platform brings contract documents, structured data, and AI-assisted analysis together within a single application. It allows users to ask questions in natural language and receive answers supported by relevant contract information, structured records, source references, and confidence indicators.

The objective is to make provider contract information easier to search, compare, analyse, and verify while reducing the effort required for manual document review.

2. Business Challenge
Provider contracts define important financial, operational, and regulatory arrangements between a health plan and its provider network. These contracts contain reimbursement rates, payment methodologies, capitation arrangements, stop-loss provisions, carve-out conditions, delegation responsibilities, compliance obligations, renewal conditions, termination requirements, and several other important terms.

This information is valuable, but it is difficult to use consistently because it is primarily stored in unstructured documents.

Contract information may be distributed across base agreements, amendments, exhibits, and supporting schedules. Users may need to review several document versions before they can identify the term that is currently applicable.

Manual review of long PDF documents also requires significant time and specialist knowledge. Users must understand document naming conventions, amendment sequences, provider relationships, and contractual terminology before they can interpret the available information accurately.

Traditional document repositories are useful for storing and retrieving files, but they do not easily support questions that require comparisons across multiple providers or contracts. For example, it may be difficult to identify which providers contain a particular clause, which contracts have upcoming deadlines, or how payment arrangements differ across the provider network.

Another important challenge is trust. A contract intelligence solution must do more than produce a fluent response. Users need to understand which information was used, which document supports the answer, and whether the response is consistent with the available evidence.

3. Proposed Solution
The Contract Intelligence Platform converts provider contract documents into a connected intelligence layer that combines structured information with searchable contract text.

The solution uses digital text extraction and Optical Character Recognition to process both digital and scanned documents. Relevant information is then organised into a contract-focused data model that covers areas such as reimbursement terms, clauses, provider identities, amendments, compliance requirements, delegation arrangements, deadlines, and operational risks.

The platform combines Generative AI, semantic search, vector search, natural-language-to-SQL capabilities, and specialized analytical tools. This allows the application to select different methods depending on the type of question being asked.

A question about a reimbursement rate may be answered using structured rate data. A question about contractual language may require retrieval of the relevant contract passage. A question about amendment history may require information from several connected documents.

The platform is therefore designed to support both structured analysis and document-level interpretation rather than functioning only as a document search or summarisation tool.

 

