4. Key Capabilities
Natural-Language Contract Search
Users can ask contract-related questions in plain English instead of manually searching through several documents and tables.

The application is designed to answer questions such as:

What are the current reimbursement terms for a provider?

What changed in the latest contract amendment?

Does the contract contain an offset clause?

Which party is responsible for a particular service?

What notice period applies to contract termination?

Which contracts have upcoming renewal dates?

How do the contractual arrangements of two providers differ?

The system determines the type of question and selects the most appropriate source of information before generating the response.

Provider-Level Intelligence
The Provider Explorer brings together important information related to an individual provider within one view.

A provider profile can include contract documents, amendment history, payment arrangements, reimbursement information, important clauses, delegation terms, compliance information, deadlines, provider identifiers, health-system relationships, and service-area information.

This gives users a common starting point for understanding a provider before performing more detailed analysis.

Financial and Rate Analysis
The platform is designed to support the analysis of current and historical reimbursement information.

Users can review payment methodologies, rate units, rate changes, escalation provisions, stop-loss arrangements, carve-out conditions, and other financial terms.

The structured data can also support comparisons between providers and scenario-based analysis after the relevant business rules and data have been validated.

Clause Intelligence
The platform extracts and organises important legal and operational clauses from provider contracts.

These may include termination provisions, auto-renewal terms, offset rights, audit rights, dispute-resolution clauses, confidentiality requirements, quality-reporting obligations, claims-submission requirements, and continuity-of-care provisions.

Users can retrieve the relevant clause text, review the supporting document, and compare similar provisions across multiple providers.

Amendment Intelligence
Provider contracts often change through multiple amendments. The platform is designed to preserve the relationship between the base agreement and subsequent amendments.

This allows users to review the amendment sequence, identify which document introduced a change, and distinguish current terms from superseded information.

This capability is particularly important when the answer to a question depends on several contract documents rather than one isolated passage.

Delegation and Responsibility Analysis
The platform organises information related to delegated functions and the Division of Financial Responsibility.

Users can review which party is responsible for claims processing, credentialing, utilisation management, prior authorisation, appeals, quality activities, member services, and specific categories of healthcare services.

This makes complex responsibility arrangements easier to understand and compare.

Compliance and Notification Intelligence
The platform can organise contractual compliance obligations and notification requirements into structured information.

Users can review notice periods, termination requirements, renewal conditions, audit provisions, quality-program obligations, and other compliance-related terms.

The application provides access to supporting contract language so that users can verify the information and continue to follow established legal and compliance review processes.

Contract Deadlines and Risks
The application includes views for important contractual dates and potential areas requiring attention.

These may include contract expirations, renewal windows, termination-notice dates, rate-effective dates, compliance deadlines, and other contractual events.

After the business rules and ownership processes have been validated, these capabilities can support more proactive contract management.

Source Verification
Source traceability is a central part of the platform.

Where the required information is available, the response can include the name of the source document, the relevant page number, the supporting passage, the structured data used, and a confidence indicator.

This allows users to review the evidence behind an answer instead of relying only on the generated response.

